### ============================================================
### Digital Wallet — Manual API Tests
### ============================================================

@host = http://localhost:8080
@email = fiza@example.com
@phone = 9876543210
@password = MyPass123

### ------------------------------------------------------------
### 7.6 Register new user (expect 201 Created)
### ------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{host}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "fullName": "Fiza Khan",
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "phoneNumber": "{{phone}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}


### ------------------------------------------------------------
### 7.7 Login (expect 200 OK) — also captures JWT into {{token}}
### ------------------------------------------------------------
# @name login
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

> {%
    client.global.set("token", response.body.token);
    client.log("JWT captured: " + response.body.token);
%}


### ------------------------------------------------------------
### 7.8 Login with WRONG password (expect 401 Unauthorized)
### ------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "WrongPass999"
}


### ------------------------------------------------------------
### 7.9 Register with DUPLICATE email (expect 409 Conflict)
### ------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{host}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "fullName": "Fiza Khan Again",
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "phoneNumber": "9876543299",
  "password": "MyPass123"
}


### ------------------------------------------------------------
### BONUS: Sanity check — hit a protected endpoint WITHOUT a token
### (expect 401 Unauthorized, because /api/auth/** is public but
###  anything else requires JWT — we'll add real endpoints in Step 8)
### ------------------------------------------------------------
GET {{host}}/api/wallets/me


### ------------------------------------------------------------
### BONUS: Sanity check — hit a protected endpoint WITH the JWT
### (expect 404 for now — endpoint doesn't exist yet, but you'll see
###  it passes Spring Security. Proof that JWT works.)
### ------------------------------------------------------------
GET {{host}}/api/wallets/me
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### ------------------------------------------------------------
### EXTRA: Register with invalid data (expect 400 with field errors)
### Tests the validation from Step 4 + global handler from Step 5
### ------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{host}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "fullName": "X",
  "email": "not-an-email",
  "phoneNumber": "abc",
  "password": "short"
}


### ============================================================
### STEP 8 — Wallet endpoints (protected)
### ============================================================

### 8.1 Get my wallet (expect 200, balance = 0)
GET {{host}}/api/wallets/me
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 8.2 Get my balance (expect 200)
GET {{host}}/api/wallets/me/balance
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 8.3 Top up 500 (expect 200, balance = 500)
POST {{host}}/api/wallets/me/topup
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "amount": 500.00
}


### 8.4 Get my wallet again (expect balance = 500)
GET {{host}}/api/wallets/me
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 8.5 Top up with negative amount (expect 400)
POST {{host}}/api/wallets/me/topup
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "amount": -10
}


### 8.6 Get my wallet without token (expect 401)
GET {{host}}/api/wallets/me



### ============================================================
### STEP 9 — P2P Transfers
### ============================================================

### 9.0 Register second user (Bob)
POST {{host}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "fullName": "Bob Kumar",
  "email": "bob@example.com",
  "phoneNumber": "9876500001",
  "password": "BobPass123"
}


### 9.1 Login as Bob, capture second token
# @name loginBob
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "bob@example.com",
  "password": "BobPass123"
}

> {%
    client.global.set("bobToken", response.body.token);
    client.log("Bob JWT: " + response.body.token);
%}


### 9.2 Bob's wallet — copy the walletNumber from the response
GET {{host}}/api/wallets/me
Authorization: Bearer {{bobToken}}

> {%
    client.global.set("bobWallet", response.body.walletNumber);
    client.log("Bob wallet number: " + response.body.walletNumber);
%}


### 9.3 (Log back in as Fiza to get fresh token for sender flow)
# @name loginFiza
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "fiza@example.com",
  "password": "MyPass123"
}

> {%
    client.global.set("token", response.body.token);
%}


### 9.4 Top up Fiza's wallet to 1000 so she has funds
POST {{host}}/api/wallets/me/topup
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "amount": 1000.00
}


### 9.5 Fiza transfers 250 to Bob (expect 200 + transaction)
POST {{host}}/api/wallets/me/transfer
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "receiverWalletNumber": "{{bobWallet}}",
  "amount": 250.00,
  "description": "Dinner split",
  "idempotencyKey": "fiza-to-bob-001"
}


### 9.6 Fiza tries the SAME transfer again with the SAME idempotency key
### (expect 200 + same transaction, NOT a double-charge)
POST {{host}}/api/wallets/me/transfer
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "receiverWalletNumber": "{{bobWallet}}",
  "amount": 250.00,
  "description": "Dinner split",
  "idempotencyKey": "fiza-to-bob-001"
}


### 9.7 Fiza's balance (expect 1000 - 250 = 750, NOT 500)
GET {{host}}/api/wallets/me/balance
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 9.8 Bob's balance (expect 250)
GET {{host}}/api/wallets/me/balance
Authorization: Bearer {{bobToken}}


### 9.9 Transfer more than balance (expect 400 Insufficient balance)
POST {{host}}/api/wallets/me/transfer
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "receiverWalletNumber": "{{bobWallet}}",
  "amount": 999999.00,
  "description": "Too much"
}


### 9.10 Transfer to yourself (expect 400)
POST {{host}}/api/wallets/me/transfer
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "receiverWalletNumber": "wrong-wallet-number",
  "amount": 10.00
}


### ============================================================
### STEP 10 — Transaction history
### ============================================================

### 10.1 Default — first page, last 30 days
GET {{host}}/api/transactions/me
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 10.2 Page size = 2
GET {{host}}/api/transactions/me?page=0&size=2
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 10.3 Second page
GET {{host}}/api/transactions/me?page=1&size=2
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 10.4 Filter by type = TRANSFER
GET {{host}}/api/transactions/me?type=TRANSFER
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 10.5 Filter by type = TOP_UP
GET {{host}}/api/transactions/me?type=TOP_UP
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 10.6 Filter by status = SUCCESS
GET {{host}}/api/transactions/me?status=SUCCESS
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 10.7 Filter by type AND status
GET {{host}}/api/transactions/me?type=TRANSFER&status=SUCCESS
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 10.8 Filter by date range (only today)
GET {{host}}/api/transactions/me?from=2026-09-29T00:00:00&to=2026-09-29T23:59:59
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


### 10.9 No token — expect 401
GET {{host}}/api/transactions/me


### 10.10 Bad enum value — expect 400
GET {{host}}/api/transactions/me?type=NOT_A_TYPE
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}